      ut":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.3
      seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"Generating a user access token for a GitHub
      App - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/generat
      ing-a-user-access-token-for-a-github-app)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;
      Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0search4\",\"lineno\":null}); Total
      lines: 218\nL17: ## In this article\nL18: \nL19: * cite5†About user access tokens L20: *
      cite6†Using the web application flow to generate a user access token L21: * cite7†Using
      the device flow to generate a user access token L22: * cite8†Generating a user access token
      when a user installs your app L23: * cite9†Using a refresh token to generate a user access
      token L24: * cite10†Troubleshooting L25: ## cite5†About user access tokens L26: \nL27:
      Note\nL28: \nL29: User access tokens that expire are currently an optional feature and are
      subject to change. To opt in or out of the token expiration feature, see cite11†Activating
      optional features for GitHub Apps . For more information, see cite12†Expiring
      user-to-server access tokens for GitHub Apps†developer.github.com .\nL31: A user access token
      is a type of OAuth token. Unlike a traditional OAuth token, the user access token does not use
      scopes. Instead, it uses fine-grained permissions. A user access token only has permissions
      that both the user and the app have. For example, if the app was granted permission to write
      the contents of a repository, but the user can only read the contents, then the user access
      token can only read the contents.\nL32: Similarly, a user access token can only access
      resources that both the user and app can access. For example, if an app is granted access to
      repository `A` and `B`, and the user can access repository `B` and `C`, the user access token
      can access repository `B` but not `A` or `C`. You can use the REST API to check which
      installations and which repositories within an installation a user access token can
      access.\nL34: When you make API requests with a user access token, the rate limits for user
      access tokens apply. For more information, see cite15†Rate limits for GitHub Apps .\nL35:
      \nL36: By default, the user access token expires after 8 hours. You can use a refresh token to
      regenerate a user access token. For more information, see cite16†Refreshing user access
      tokens .\nL37: Users can revoke their authorization of a GitHub App. For more information,
      see cite17†Token expiration and revocation . If a user revokes their authorization of a
      GitHub App, the app will receive the `github_app_authorization` webhook. GitHub Apps cannot
      unsubscribe from this event. If your app receives this webhook, you should stop calling the
      API on behalf of the user who revoked the token. If your app continues to use a revoked access
      token, it will receive the `401 Bad Credentials` error.\nL38: For more information about this
      webhook, see cite18†Webhook events and payloads .\nL39: You should keep user access tokens
      and refresh tokens secure. For more information, see cite19†Best practices for creating a
      GitHub App .\nL40: ## cite6†Using the web application flow to generate a user access token
      L41: \nL42: If your app runs in the browser, you should use the web application flow to
      generate a user access token. For a tutorial about using the web application flow, see
      cite20†Building a \"Login with GitHub\" button with a GitHub App .\nL43: 1. Direct the user
      to this URL, and add any necessary query parameters from the following list of parameters:
      `https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize`. For example, this URL specifies the `client_id`
      and `state` parameters:
      `https://github.com/login/oauth/authorize?client_id=12345&state=abcdefg`.\nL44: Query
      parameter | Type | Required? | Description\nL45: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL46: `client_id` |
      `string` | Required | The client ID for your GitHub App. The client ID is different from the
      app ID. You can find the client ID on the settings page for your app. For more information
      about navigating to the settings page for your GitHub App, see cite21†Modifying a GitHub App
      registration .\nL47: `redirect_uri` | `string` | Strongly recommended | The URL in your
      application where users will be sent after authorization. This must be an exact match to one
      of the URLs you provided as a \"Callback URL\" in your app's settings and can't contain any
      additional parameters.\nL48: `state` | `string` | Strongly recommended | When specified, the
      value should contain a random string to protect against forgery attacks, and it can also
      contain any other arbitrary data.\nL49: `code_challenge` | `string` | Strongly recommended |
      Used to secure the authentication flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange). Required if
      `code_challenge_method` is included. Must be a 43 character SHA-256 hash of a random string
      generated by the client. See the cite22†PKCE RFC†datatracker.ietf.org for more details
      about this security extension.\nL50: `code_challenge_method` | `string` | Strongly recommended
      | Used to secure the authentication flow with PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange). Required if
      `code_challenge` is included. Must be `S256` - the `plain` code challenge method is not
      supported.\nL51: `login` | `string` | Optional | When specified, the web application flow will
      prompt users with a specific account they can use for signing in and authorizing your
      app.\nL52: `allow_signup` | `boolean` | Optional | Whether unauthenticated users will be
      offered an option to sign up for GitHub during the OAuth flow. The default is `true`. Use
      `false` when a policy prohibits signups.\nL53: `prompt` | `string` | Optional | Forces the
      account picker to appear if set to `select_account`. The account picker will also appear if
      the application has a non-HTTP redirect URI or if the user has multiple accounts signed
      in.\nL54: 2. If the user accepts your authorization request, GitHub will redirect the user to
      one of the callback URLs in your app settings, and provide a `code` query parameter you can
      use in the next step to create a user access token. If you specified `redirect_uri` in the
      previous step, that callback URL will be used. Otherwise, the first callback URL on your app's
      settings page will be used.\nL55: If you specified the `state` parameter in the previous step,
      GitHub will also include a `state` parameter. If the `state` parameter does not match the
      `state` parameter that you sent in the previous step, the request cannot be trusted, and the
