      App - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/generat
      ing-a-user-access-token-for-a-github-app)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;
      Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":83}); Total lines:
      218\nL67: Response parameter | Type | Description\nL68: --- | --- | ---\nL69: `access_token` |
      `string` | The user access token. The token starts with `ghu_`.\nL70: `expires_in` | `integer`
      | The number of seconds until `access_token` expires. If you disabled expiration of user
      access tokens, this parameter will be omitted. The value will always be `28800` (8
      hours).\nL71: `refresh_token` | `string` | The refresh token. If you disabled expiration of
      user access tokens, this parameter will be omitted. The token starts with `ghr_`.\nL72:
      `refresh_token_expires_in` | `integer` | The number of seconds until `refresh_token` expires.
      If you disabled expiration of user access tokens, this parameter will be omitted. The value
      will always be `15897600` (6 months).\nL73: `scope` | `string` | The scopes that the token
      has. This value will always be an empty string. Unlike a traditional OAuth token, the user
      access token is limited to the permissions that both your app and the user have.\nL74:
      `token_type` | `string` | The type of token. The value will always be `bearer`.\nL75: 5. Use
      the user access token from the previous step to make API requests on behalf of the user.
      Include the user access token in the `Authorization` header of an API request. For
      example:\nL76: \nL77: `curl --request GET \\\nL78: --url \"https://api.github.com/user\"
      \\\nL79: --header \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\nL80: --header \"Authorization:
      Bearer [REDACTED_TOKEN]\" \\\nL81: --header \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\"\nL82: `\nL83:
      ## cite7†Using the device flow to generate a user access token L84: \nL85: If your app is
      headless or does not have access to a browser, you should use the device flow to generate a
      user access token. For example, CLI tools, simple Raspberry Pis, and desktop applications
      should use the device flow. For a tutorial that uses device flow, see cite23†Building a CLI
      with a GitHub App .\nL86: Before you can use the device flow, you must first enable it in
      your app's settings. For more information on enabling device flow, see cite24†Modifying a
      GitHub App registration .\nL87: \nL88: The device flow uses the cite25†OAuth 2.0 Device
      Authorization Grant†datatracker.ietf.org .\nL89: 1. Send a `POST` request to
      `https://github.com/login/device/code` along with a `client_id` query parameter. The client ID
      is different from the app ID. You can find the client ID on the settings page for your app.
      For more information about navigating to the settings page for your GitHub App, see
      cite21†Modifying a GitHub App registration .\nL90: \nL91: 2. GitHub will give a response
      that includes the following query parameters:\nL92: Response parameter | Type |
      Description\nL93: --- | --- | ---\nL94: `device_code` | `string` | A verification code that is
      used to verify the device. This code is 40 characters long.\nL95: `user_code` | `string` | A
      verification code that your application should display so that the user can enter the code in
      a browser. This code is 8 characters with a hyphen in the middle. For example,
      `WDJB-MJHT`.\nL96: `verification_uri` | `string` | The URL where users need to enter their
      `user_code`. The URL is: cite26†`https://github.com/login/device`†github.com .\nL97:
      `expires_in` | `integer` | The number of seconds before the `device_code` and `user_code`
      expire. The default is 900 seconds (15 minutes).\nL98: `interval` | `integer` | The minimum
      number of seconds that must pass before you can make a new access token request (`POST
      https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token`) to complete the device authorization. If you
      make a request before this interval passes, then you will hit the rate limit and receive a
      `slow_down` error. The default is 5 seconds.\nL99: 3. Prompt the user to enter the `user_code`
      from the previous step at cite26†`https://github.com/login/device`†github.com .\nL100: If
      the user does not enter the code before the `expires_in` time passes, the code will be
      invalid. In this case, you should restart the device flow.\nL101: \nL102: 4. Poll `POST
      https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token` along with the `client_id`, `device_code`, and
      `grant_type` query parameters (described below) until the device and user codes expire or the
      user has successfully authorized the app by entering the `user_code`.\nL103: Query parameter |
      Type | Description\nL104: --- | --- | ---\nL105: `client_id` | `string` | Required. The client
      ID for your GitHub App.\nL106: `device_code` | `string` | Required. The device verification
      code you received in the previous step.\nL107: `grant_type` | `string` | Required. The grant
      type must be `urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code`.\nL108: `repository_id` | `string`
      | The ID of a single repository that the user access token can access. If the GitHub App or
      user cannot access the repository, this will be ignored. Use this parameter to restrict the
      access of the user access token further.\nL109: Do not poll this endpoint at a higher
      frequency than the frequency indicated by `interval`. If you do, you will hit the rate limit
      and receive a `slow_down` error. The `slow_down` error response adds 5 seconds to the last
      `interval`.\nL110: \nL111: Until the user enters the code, GitHub will respond with a 200
      status and an `error` response query
      parameter.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n
      Refreshing user access tokens - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/refresh
      ing-user-access-tokens)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type:
      text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"lineno\":24}); Total lines: 80\nL0:
      Collapse sidebar Expand sidebar\nL1: \nL2: Scroll breadcrumbs left\nL3: \nL4: 1. cite0†Home
      L5: 2. cite1†Apps L6: 3. cite2†Creating GitHub Apps L7: 4. cite3†Authenticate with a
      GitHub App L8: 5. cite4†Refresh user access tokens L9: \nL10: Scroll breadcrumbs
      right\nL11: \nL12: # Refreshing user access tokens\nL13: \nL14: To enforce regular token
      rotation and reduce the impact of a compromised token, you can configure your GitHub App to
      use user access tokens that expire.\nL15: \nL16: Copy as Markdown\nL17: ## In this
      article\nL18: \nL19: * cite5†About user access tokens that expire L20: *
      cite6†Configuring your app to use user access tokens that expire L21: * cite7†Refreshing
      a user access token with a refresh token L22: ## cite5†About user access tokens that
      expire L23: \nL24: Note\nL25: \nL26: User access tokens that expire are currently an optional
      feature and are subject to change. For more information, see cite8†Expiring user-to-server
      access tokens for GitHub Apps†developer.github.com .\nL27: To enforce regular token rotation
      and reduce the impact of a compromised token, you can configure your GitHub App to use user
      access tokens that expire. If your app uses user access tokens that expire, then you will
      receive a refresh token when you generate a user access token. The user access token expires
      after eight hours, and the refresh token expires after six months. For more information, see
      cite9†Generating a user access token for a GitHub App .\nL28: You can use the refresh token
      to generate a new user access token and a new refresh token. Once you use a refresh token,
      that refresh token and the old user access token will no longer work.\nL29: \nL30: If your
      refresh token expires before you use it, you can regenerate a user access token and refresh
      token by sending users through the web application flow or device flow. For more information,
      see cite9†Generating a user access token for a GitHub App .\nL31: ## cite6†Configuring
      your app to use user access tokens that expire L32: \nL33: When you create your app,
      expiration of user access tokens is enabled unless you opt out. For more information, see
      cite10†Registering a GitHub App . You can also configure this setting after your app has
      been created.\nL34: \nL35: 1. In the upper-right corner of any page on GitHub, click your
      profile picture.\nL36: \nL37: 2. Navigate to your account settings.\nL38: * For an app owned
      by a personal account, click Settings.\nL39: * For an app owned by an organization:\nL40: 1.
      Click Your organizations.\nL41: 2. To the right of the organization, click Settings.\nL42: 3.
      In the left sidebar, click Developer settings.\nL43: \nL44: 4. In the left sidebar, click
      GitHub Apps.\nL45: \nL46: 5. Next to the GitHub App that you want to modify, click Edit.\nL47:
      \nL48: 6. In the GitHub Apps settings sidebar, click Optional Features.\nL49: 7. Next to
      \"User-to-server token expiration\", click Opt-in or Opt-out. This setting may take a couple
      of seconds to apply.\nL50: \nL51: GitHub recommends that you opt in to this feature for
      improved security.\nL52: If you opt into user access tokens that expire after you have already
      generated user access tokens, the previously generated user access tokens will not expire. You
      can delete these tokens by using the `DELETE /applications/CLIENT_ID/token` endpoint. For more
      information, see cite11†REST API endpoints for OAuth authorizations .\nL53: ##
      cite7†Refreshing a user access token with a refresh token L54: \nL55: 1. Make a `POST`
      request to this URL, along with the following query parameters:
      `https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token`\nL56: Query parameter | Type | Description\nL57:
      --- | --- | ---\nL58: `client_id` | `string` | Required. The client ID for your GitHub App.
      The client ID is different from the app ID. You can find the client ID on the settings page
      for your app.\nL59: `client_secret` | `string` | Required unless the user access token was
      generated using the device flow. The client secret for your GitHub App.\nL60: `grant_type` |
      `string` | Required. The value must be \"refresh_token\".\nL61: `refresh_token` | `string` |
      Required. The refresh token that you received when you generated a user access token.\nL62: 2.
      GitHub will give a response that includes the following parameters:\nL63: Response parameter |
      Type | Description\nL64: --- | --- |
      ---\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGenerat
      ing an installation access token for a GitHub App - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/generat
      ing-an-installation-access-token-for-a-github-app?WT.mc_id=DOP-MVP-4039670)\nciteturn2view2
      [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source:
      open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view3\",\"lineno\":271}); Total lines: 328\nL259: ## About
      installation access tokens\nL260: \nL261: In order to authenticate as an app installation, you
      must generate an installation access token. For more information about authenticating as an
      app installation, see cite31†Authenticating as a GitHub App installation .\nL262: \nL263:
      Note\nL264: Instead of generating an installation access token, you can use GitHub's Octokit
      SDKs to authenticate as an app. The SDK will take care of generating an installation access
      token for you and will regenerate the token once it expires. For more information about
      authenticating as an app installation, see cite31†Authenticating as a GitHub App
      installation .\nL265: \nL266: You should keep your installation access token secure. For more
      information, see cite19†Best practices for creating a GitHub App .\nL267: \nL268:
      Note\nL269: Starting April 27, 2026, GitHub began a staged rollout of a stateless format
      (`ghs_APPID_JWT`) to all newly minted GitHub App installation tokens, making them more
      performant and improving the reliability of our API surface. If your application expects or
      relies on installation tokens being exactly 40 characters long, it may not handle this new
      token format correctly. You can now validate your apps and workflows using a temporary request
      header that lets you enable the token format on demand.\nL270: For more information about the
      temporary header, see cite99†the GitHub blog†github.blog .\nL271: ## Generating an
      installation access token\nL272: \nL273: 1. Generate a JSON web token (JWT) for your app. For
      more information, see cite34†Generating a JSON Web Token (JWT) for a GitHub App .\nL274:
      \nL275: 2. Get the ID of the installation that you want to authenticate as.\nL276: \nL277: If
      you are responding to a webhook event, the webhook payload will include the installation
      ID.\nL278: You can also use the REST API to find the ID for an installation of your app. For
      example, you can get an installation ID with the `GET /users/{username}/installation`, `GET
      /repos/{owner}/{repo}/installation`, `GET /orgs/{org}/installation`, or `GET
      /app/installations` endpoints. For more information, see cite100†REST API endpoints for
      GitHub Apps .\nL279: You can also find the app ID on the settings page for your app. The app
      ID is different from the client ID. For more information about navigating to the settings page
      for your GitHub App, see cite101†Modifying a GitHub App registration .\nL280: \nL281: 3.
      Send a REST API `POST` request to `/app/installations/INSTALLATION_ID/access_tokens`. Include
      your JSON web token in the `Authorization` header of your request. Replace `INSTALLATION_ID`
      with the ID of the installation that you want to authenticate as.\nL282: For example, send
      this curl request. Replace `INSTALLATION_ID` with the ID of the installation and `JWT` with
      your JSON web token:\nL283: \nL284: curl --request POST \\\nL285: --url
      \"https://api.github.com/app/installations/INSTALLATION_ID/access_tokens\" \\\nL286: --header
      \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\\nL287: --header \"Authorization: Bearer JWT\"
      \\\nL288: --header \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\"\nL289: Optionally, you can use the
      `repositories` or `repository_ids` body parameters to specify individual repositories that the
      installation access token can access. If you don't use `repositories` or `repository_ids` to
      grant access to specific repositories, the installation access token will have access to all
      repositories that the installation was granted access to. The installation access token cannot
      be granted access to repositories that the installation was not granted access to.\nL290: You
      can list up to 500 repositories.\nL291: Optionally, use the `permissions` body parameter to
      specify the permissions that the installation access token should have. If `permissions` is
      not specified, the installation access token will have all of the permissions that were
      granted to the app. The installation access token cannot be granted permissions that the app
      was not granted.\nL292: The response will include an installation access token, the time that
      the token expires, the permissions that the token has, and the repositories that the token can
      access, if applicable. The installation access token will expire after 1 hour.\nL293: \nL294:
      For more information about this endpoint, see cite100†REST API endpoints for GitHub Apps
      .\nL295: \nL296: Note\nL297: In most cases, you can use `Authorization: Bearer` or
      `Authorization: token` to pass a token. However, if you are passing a JSON web token (JWT),
      you must use `Authorization: Bearer`.\nL298: ## Help and support\nL299: \nL300: ### Did you
      find what you needed?\nL301: \nL302: Yes No\nL303: \nL304: cite102†Privacy policy L305:
      \nL306: ### Help us make these docs great!\nL307: \nL308: All GitHub docs are open source. See
      something that's wrong or unclear? Submit a pull request.\nL309: \nL310: cite103†Make a
      contribution†github.com L311: \nL312: cite104†Learn how to contribute L313: \nL314: ###
      Still need help?\nL315: \nL316: cite105†Ask the GitHub community†github.com L317: \nL318:
      cite106†Contact support†support.github.com
      --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nManaging
      private keys for GitHub Apps - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/authenticating-with-a-github-app/managin
      g-private-keys-for-github-apps)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content
      type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view4\",\"lineno\":24}); Total lines:
      101\nL0: Collapse sidebar Expand sidebar\nL1: \nL2: Scroll breadcrumbs left\nL3: \nL4: 1.
      cite0†Home L5: 2. cite1†Apps L6: 3. cite2†Creating GitHub Apps L7: 4.
      cite3†Authenticate with a GitHub App L8: 5. cite4†Manage private keys L9: \nL10: Scroll
      breadcrumbs right\nL11: \nL12: # Managing private keys for GitHub Apps\nL13: \nL14: You can
      manage private keys to authenticate with your GitHub App.\nL15: \nL16: Copy as Markdown\nL17:
      ## In this article\nL18: \nL19: * cite5†About private keys for GitHub Apps L20: *
      cite6†Generating private keys L21: * cite7†Verifying private keys L22: *
      cite8†Deleting private keys L23: * cite9†Storing private keys L24: ## cite5†About
      private keys for GitHub Apps L25: \nL26: After you create a GitHub App, you'll need to
      generate a private key in order to make requests to the GitHub API as the application itself.
      For example, you need a private key to sign a JSON Web Token (JWT) in order to request an
      installation access token. For more information, see cite10†Generating a JSON Web Token
      (JWT) for a GitHub App L27: You can create up to 25 private keys for an app. You should use
      multiple keys in order to rotate keys without downtime in the event of a key compromise. If
      your application has 25 or more keys, you must delete some before you can create more.\nL28:
      \nL29: Private keys do not expire and instead need to be manually revoked. For more
      information about how to revoke or delete a private key, see cite8†Deleting private keys
      .\nL30: You must keep private keys for GitHub Apps secure. For more information, see
      cite9†Storing private keys .\nL31: \nL32: To verify that a private key matches a public
      key, see cite7†Verifying private keys .\nL33: ## cite6†Generating private keys L34:
      \nL35: To generate a private key:\nL36: \nL37: 1. In the upper-right corner of any page on
      GitHub, click your profile picture.\nL38: \nL39: 2. Navigate to your account settings.\nL40:
      \nL41: * For an app owned by a personal account, click Settings.\nL42: * For an app owned by
      an organization:\nL43: 1. Click Your organizations.\nL44: 2. To the right of the organization,
      click Settings.\nL45: 3. In the left sidebar, click Developer settings.\nL46: \nL47: 4. In the
      left sidebar, click GitHub Apps.\nL48: 5. Next to the GitHub App that you want to generate a
      private key for, click Edit.\nL49: \nL50: 6. Under \"Private keys\", click Generate a private
      key.\nL51: \nL52: 7. You will see a private key in PEM format downloaded to your computer.
      Make sure to store this file because GitHub only stores the public portion of the key. For
      more information about securely storing your key, see cite9†Storing private keys .\nL53:
      \nL54: Note\nL55: If you're using a library that requires a specific file format, the PEM file
      you download will be in `PKCS#1 RSAPrivateKey` format.\nL56: ## cite7†Verifying private
      keys L57: \nL58: GitHub generates a fingerprint for each private and public key pair using
      the SHA-256 hash function. You can verify that your private key matches the public key stored
      on GitHub by generating the fingerprint of your private key and comparing it to the
      fingerprint shown on GitHub.\nL59: \nL60: To verify a private key:\nL61: 1. Find the
      fingerprint for the private and public key pair you want to verify in the \"Private keys\"
      section of the settings page for your GitHub App. For more information, see cite6†Generating
      private keys .\nL62: \nL63: cite11†Image: Screenshot of a private key in a GitHub App
      settings page. The fingerprint, the part of the private key after the colon, is outlined in
      dark orange. L64: 2. Generate the fingerprint of your private key (PEM) locally by using the
      following command:\nL65: \nL66: `openssl rsa -in PATH_TO_PEM_FILE -pubout -outform DER |
      openssl sha256 -binary | openssl base64\nL67: `\nL68: \nL69: 3. Compare the results of the
      locally generated fingerprint to the fingerprint you see in GitHub.\nL70: ## cite8†Deleting
      private keys L71: \nL72: You can remove a lost or compromised private key by deleting it, but
      you must regenerate a new key before you can delete the existing key.\nL73: 1. In the
      upper-right corner of any page on GitHub, click your profile picture.\nL74: 2. Navigate to
      your account settings.\nL75: * For an app owned by a personal account, click Settings.\nL76: *
      For an app owned by an organization:\nL77: 1. Click Your organizations.\nL78: 2. To the right
      of the organization, click Settings.\nL79: 3. In the left sidebar, click Developer
      settings.\nL80: 4. In the left sidebar, click GitHub Apps.\nL81: 5. Next to the GitHub App
      that you want to delete a private key for, click
      Edit.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChoos
      ing permissions for a GitHub App - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/creating-github-apps/registering-a-github-app/choosing-permis
      sions-for-a-github-app?apiVersion=2022-11-28)\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled:
      today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"lineno\":24}); Total
      lines: 80\nL0: Collapse sidebar Expand sidebar\nL1: \nL2: Scroll breadcrumbs left\nL3: \nL4:
      1. cite0†Home L5: 2. cite1†Apps L6: 3. cite2†Creating GitHub Apps L7: 4.
      cite3†Registering a GitHub App L8: 5. cite4†Permissions L9: \nL10: Scroll breadcrumbs
      right\nL11: \nL12: # Choosing permissions for a GitHub App\nL13: \nL14: The permissions of a
      GitHub App determine what the app can do with GitHub's APIs and what webhooks the app can
      receive.\nL15: \nL16: Copy as Markdown\nL17: ## In this article\nL18: \nL19: * cite5†About
      GitHub App permissions L20: * cite6†About changes to permissions L21: * cite7†Choosing
      permissions for webhook access L22: * cite8†Choosing permissions for REST API access L23:
      * cite9†Choosing permissions for GraphQL API access L24: * cite10†Choosing permissions
      for Git access L25: ## cite5†About GitHub App permissions L26: \nL27: GitHub Apps don't
      have any permissions by default. When you register a GitHub App, you can select permissions
      for the app. The permissions that you select determine what the app can do with GitHub's APIs
      and what webhooks the app can subscribe to. You should select the minimum permissions required
      for the app.\nL28: Although GitHub Apps don't have any permissions by default, they do have
      implicit permissions to read public resources when acting on behalf of a user. When a user
      authorizes the app to act on their behalf, the GitHub App can use the resulting user access
      token to make requests to the REST API and the GraphQL API to read public resources. To learn
      more about acting on behalf of a user, see cite11†Authenticating with a GitHub App on behalf
      of a user .\nL29: App permissions are classified as repository, organization, enterprise, or
      account permissions.\nL30: * Repository permissions allow your app to access resources related
      to repositories that are owned by the account where the app is installed.\nL31: * Organization
      permissions allow your app to access resources related to the organization where the app is
      installed, if it is installed on an organization account.\nL32: * Enterprise permissions allow
      the app to manage an enterprise, if it is installed on an enterprise account.\nL33: * Account
      permissions allow your app to access resources related to a user if the user has also
      authorized your app. For more information about user authorization of apps, see
      cite11†Authenticating with a GitHub App on behalf of a user .\nL34: Any user, organization,
      or enterprise can create an app using any of the permission types. An app with enterprise
      permissions must be `public` or `internal` to be installed on an enterprise account. A
      `public` app can be installed by any enterprise, while an `internal` app can only be installed
      by the enterprise that owns the app or the enterprise of the organization that owns the app.
      There are no additional restrictions on which organizations or personal accounts an app can be
      installed on.\nL35: There is one exception to installing an app across enterprises. If an app
      requests the \"Enterprise organization installations\" or \"Enterprise organization
      installation repositories\" permissions, the only enterprise that can install it is the one
      that owns the app. You cannot install the app on another enterprise, and you cannot add either
      permission to an app that is already installed on an enterprise other than the one that owns
      it.\nL36: When a user installs an app on their user account or organization, they see and
      grant the repository and organization permissions that the app requested. When they install it
      on their enterprise, they see and grant only the enterprise permissions that the app
      requested. They will also see a list of account permissions that the app can request for
      individual users. When a user authorizes an app to act on their behalf, they will see and
      grant the account permissions that the app requested.\nL37: The success of an API request with
      a user access token depends on the user's permissions as well as the app's permissions. For
      example, if the app was granted permission to write the contents of a repository, but the user
      can only read the contents, then the user access token can only read the contents. The success
      of an API request with an installation access token only depends on the app's
      permissions.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      \nAuthorizing OAuth apps - GitHub Docs
      (https://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps?apiVers
      ion=2022-11-28)\nciteturn2view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html;
      Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view6\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 297\nL121: ##
      cite6†Device flow L122: \nL123: The device flow allows you to authorize users for a
      headless application, such as a CLI tool or the cite25†Git Credential Manager†github.com
      .\nL124: \nL125: Before you can use the device flow to authorize and identify users, you must
      first enable it in your app's settings. For more information about enabling the device flow in
      your app, see cite26†Modifying a GitHub App registration for GitHub Apps and
      cite27†Modifying an OAuth app for OAuth apps.\nL126: ### cite28†Overview of the device
      flow L127: \nL128: 1. Your app requests device and user verification codes and gets the
      authorization URL where the user will enter the user verification code.\nL129: 2. The app
      prompts the user to enter a user verification code at
      cite29†`https://github.com/login/device`†github.com .\nL130: 3. The app polls for the user
      authentication status. Once the user has authorized the device, the app will be able to make
      API calls with a new access token.\nL131: ### cite30†Step 1: App requests the device and
      user verification codes from GitHub L132: \nL133: `POST
      https://github.com/login/device/code\nL134: `\nL135: \nL136: Your app must request a user
      verification code and verification URL that the app will use to prompt the user to
      authenticate in the next step. This request also returns a device verification code that the
      app must use to receive an access token and check the status of user authentication.\nL137:
      \nL138: The endpoint takes the following input parameters.\nL139: Parameter name | Type |
      Description\nL140: --- | --- | ---\nL141: `client_id` | `string` | Required. The client ID you
      received from GitHub for your app.\nL142: `scope` | `string` | A space-delimited list of the
      scopes that your app is requesting access to. For more information, see cite21†Scopes for
      OAuth apps .\nL143: By default, the response takes the following form:\nL144: \nL145:
      `device_code=3584d83530557fdd1f46af8289938c8ef79f9dc5&expires_in=900&interval=5&user_code=WDJB
      -MJHT&verification_uri=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Flogin%2Fdevice\nL146: `\nL147: Parameter
      name | Type | Description\nL148: --- | --- | ---\nL149: `device_code` | `string` | The device
      verification code is 40 characters and used to verify the device.\nL150: `user_code` |
      `string` | The user verification code is displayed on the device so the user can enter the
      code in a browser. This code is 8 characters with a hyphen in the middle.\nL151:
      `verification_uri` | `string` | The verification URL where users need to enter the
      `user_code`: cite29†`https://github.com/login/device`†github.com .\nL152: `expires_in` |
      `integer` | The number of seconds before the `device_code` and `user_code` expire. The default
      is 900 seconds or 15 minutes.\nL153: `interval` | `integer` | The minimum number of seconds
      that must pass before you can make a new access token request (`POST
      https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token`) to complete the device authorization. For
      example, if the interval is 5, then you cannot make a new request until 5 seconds pass. If you
      make more than one request over 5 seconds, then you will hit the rate limit and receive a
      `slow_down` error.\nL154: You can also receive the response in different formats if you
      provide the format in the `Accept` header. For example, `Accept: application/json` or `Accept:
      application/xml`:\nL155: \nL156: `Accept: application/json\nL157: {\nL158: \"device_code\":
      \"3584d83530557fdd1f46af8289938c8ef79f9dc5\",\nL159: \"user_code\": \"WDJB-MJHT\",\nL160:
      \"verification_uri\": \"https://github.com/login/device\",\nL161: \"expires_in\": 900,\nL162:
      \"interval\": 5\nL163: }\nL164: `\nL165: \nL166: `Accept: application/xml\nL167:
      <OAuth>\nL168: <device_code>3584d83530557fdd1f46af8289938c8ef79f9dc5</device_code>\nL169:
      <user_code>WDJB-MJHT</user_code>\nL170:
      <verification_uri>https://github.com/login/device</verification_uri>\nL171:
      <expires_in>900</expires_in>\nL172: <interval>5</interval>\nL173: </OAuth>\nL174: `\nL175: ###
      cite31†Step 2: Prompt the user to enter the user code in a browser L176: \nL177: Your
      device will show the user verification code and prompt the user to enter the code at
      cite29†`https://github.com/login/device`†github.com .\nL178: ### cite32†Step 3: App polls
      GitHub to check if the user authorized the device L179: \nL180: `POST
      https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token\nL181: `\nL182: Your app will make device
      authorization requests that poll `POST https://github.com/login/oauth/access_token`, until the
      device and user codes expire or the user has successfully authorized the app with a valid user
      code. The app must use the minimum polling `interval` retrieved in step 1 to avoid rate limit
      errors. For more information, see cite33†Rate limits for the device flow .\nL183: The user
      must enter a valid code within 15 minutes (or 900 seconds). After 15 minutes, you will need to
      request a new device authorization code with `POST
      https://github.com/login/device/code`.\nL184: \nL185: Once the user has authorized, the app
      will receive an access token that can be used to make requests to the API on behalf of a
      user.\nL186: \nL187: The endpoint takes the following input parameters.\nL188: Parameter name
      | Type | Description\nL189: --- | --- | ---\nL190: `client_id` | `string` | Required. The
      client ID you received from GitHub for your OAuth app.\nL191: `device_code` | `string` |
      Required. The `device_code` you received from the `POST https://github.com/login/device/code`
      request.\nL192: `grant_type` | `string` | Required. The grant type must be
      `urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code`.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrou
