      on-and-revocation)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html;
      Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3search0\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 85\nL0: Collapse
      sidebar Expand sidebar\nL1: \nL2: Scroll breadcrumbs left\nL3: \nL4: 1. cite0†Home L5: 2.
      cite1†Authentication L6: 3. cite2†Account security L7: 4. cite3†Token expiration L8:
      \nL9: Scroll breadcrumbs right\nL10: \nL11: # Token expiration and revocation\nL12: \nL13:
      Your tokens can expire and can also be revoked by you, applications you have authorized, and
      GitHub itself.\nL14: \nL15: Copy as Markdown\nL16: ## In this article\nL17: \nL18: *
      cite4†Token revoked after reaching its expiration date L19: * cite5†Token revoked when
      pushed to a public repository or public gist L20: * cite6†Token expired due to lack of use
      L21: * cite7†Token revoked by the user L22: * cite8†Token revoked by a third party L23:
      * cite9†Token revoked by the OAuth app L24: * cite10†Token revoked due to excess of
      tokens for an OAuth app with the same scope L25: * cite11†User token expired due to GitHub
      App configuration L26: * cite12†Token revoked by enterprise owners L27: When a token has
      expired or has been revoked, it can no longer be used to authenticate Git and API requests. It
      is not possible to restore an expired or revoked token, you or the application will need to
      create a new token.\nL28: \nL29: This article explains the possible reasons your GitHub token
      might be revoked or expire.\nL30: \nL31: Note\nL32: When a personal access token, OAuth app
      token, or GitHub App token expires or is revoked, you may see an `oauth_authorization.destroy`
      action in your security log. For more information, see cite13†Reviewing your security log
      .\nL33: ## cite4†Token revoked after reaching its expiration date L34: \nL35: When you
      create a personal access token, we recommend that you set an expiration for your token. Upon
      reaching your token's expiration date, the token is automatically revoked. For more
      information, see cite14†Managing your personal access tokens .\nL36: ## cite5†Token
      revoked when pushed to a public repository or public gist L37: \nL38: If a valid OAuth token,
      GitHub App token, or personal access token is pushed to a public repository or public gist,
      the token will be automatically revoked.\nL39: \nL40: ## cite6†Token expired due to lack of
      use L41: \nL42: GitHub will automatically revoke an OAuth token or personal access token when
      the token hasn't been used in one year.\nL43: ## cite7†Token revoked by the user L44:
      \nL45: You can revoke your authorization of a GitHub App or OAuth app from your account
      settings which will revoke any tokens associated with the app. For more information, see
      cite15†Reviewing and revoking authorization of GitHub Apps and cite16†Reviewing your
      authorized OAuth apps .\nL46: Once an authorization is revoked, any tokens associated with
      the authorization will be revoked as well. To reauthorize an application, follow the
      instructions from the third-party application or website to connect your account on GitHub
      again.\nL47: \nL48: You can also revoke all your credentials at once from your account
      settings. This is useful if you believe your account may be compromised or your hardware was
      lost or stolen. For more information, see cite17†Revoking your credentials .\nL49: ##
      cite8†Token revoked by a third party L50: \nL51: To prevent unauthorized access using
      exposed tokens, GitHub recommends token revocation to ensure that a token can no longer be
      used to authenticate to GitHub. The credential revocation API supports revoking the following
      token types:\nL52: * Personal access tokens (classic) with the `ghp_` prefix\nL53: *
      Fine-grained personal access tokens with the `github_pat_` prefix\nL54: * OAuth app tokens
      with the `gho_` prefix\nL55: * GitHub App user-to-server tokens with the `ghu_` prefix\nL56: *
      GitHub App refresh tokens with the `ghr_` prefix\nL57: \nL58: If you find any of these tokens
      leaked on GitHub or elsewhere, you can submit a revocation request through the REST API. See
      cite18†Revocation for the complete and authoritative list of supported token types.\nL59:
      When a valid token is submitted to GitHub's credential revocation API, the token will be
      automatically revoked. This API allows a third party to revoke a token they do not own and
      helps protect the data associated with this token from unauthorized access, limiting the
      impact of exposed tokens.\nL60: To encourage reports and ensure that exposed tokens can be
      quickly and easily revoked, we do not require authentication for the revocation requests
      submitted through the API. As a result, GitHub is unable to provide further information about
      the source of the reported token.\nL61: ## cite9†Token revoked by the OAuth app L62: \nL63:
      The owner of an OAuth app can revoke an account's authorization of their app, this will also
      revoke any tokens associated with the authorization. For more information about revoking
      authorizations of your OAuth app, see cite19†REST API endpoints for OAuth authorizations
      .\nL64: OAuth app owners can also revoke individual tokens associated with an authorization.
      For more information about revoking individual tokens for your OAuth app, see cite20†REST
      API endpoints for OAuth authorizations .\nL65: ## cite10†Token revoked due to excess of
      tokens for an OAuth app with the same scope L66: There is a limit of ten tokens that are
      issued per user/application/scope combination, and a rate limit of ten tokens created per
      hour. If an application creates more than ten tokens for the same user and the same scopes,
      the oldest tokens with the same user/application/scope combination are revoked. However,
      hitting the hourly rate limit will not revoke your oldest token.\nL67: Instead, it will
      trigger a re-authorization prompt within the browser, asking the user to double check the
      permissions they're granting your app. This prompt is intended to give a break to any
      potential infinite loop the app is stuck in, since there's little to no reason for an app to
      request ten tokens from the user within an hour.\nL68: ## cite11†User token expired due to
      GitHub App configuration L69: \nL70: User access tokens created by a GitHub App will expire
      after eight hours by default, and then must be regenerated using the included refresh token.
      Owners of GitHub Apps can optionally configure these tokens to never expire instead, but this
      is not recommended due to the security implications. For more information about configuring
      your GitHub App's user access tokens, see cite21†Activating optional features for GitHub
      Apps .\nL71: ## cite12†Token revoked by enterprise owners L72: \nL73: Enterprise owners on
      GitHub Enterprise Cloud can revoke SSO authorizations or delete credentials for individual
      users or in bulk when responding to security incidents. Revoking SSO authorizations removes
      access to SSO-protected organization resources, while deleting credentials (available for
      Enterprise Managed Users only) removes the credentials entirely.\nL74: \nL75: For more
      information, see cite22†Revoking SSO authorizations or deleting credentials in your
      enterprise .\nL76: ## Legal\nL77: \nL78: * © 2026 GitHub, Inc.\nL79: * cite23†Terms L80: *
      cite24†Privacy L81: * cite25†Status†www.githubstatus.com L82: *
      cite26†Pricing†github.com L83: * cite27†Expert services†services.github.com L84: *
      cite28†Blog†github.blog
