      cite1†REST API L6: 3. cite2†Apps L7: 4. cite3†OAuth authorizations L8: \nL9: Scroll
      breadcrumbs right\nL10: \nL11: The REST API is now versioned. For more information, see
      \"cite4†About API versioning .\"\nL12: \nL13: # REST API endpoints for OAuth
      authorizations\nL14: \nL15: Use the REST API to interact with OAuth apps and OAuth
      authorizations of GitHub Apps\nL16: ## cite5†About OAuth apps and OAuth authorizations of
      GitHub Apps L17: \nL18: You can use these endpoints to manage the OAuth tokens that OAuth
      apps or GitHub Apps use to access people's accounts on GitHub.\nL19: \nL20: Tokens for OAuth
      apps have the prefix `gho_`, while OAuth tokens for GitHub Apps, used for authenticating on
      behalf of the user, have the prefix `ghu_`. You can use the following endpoints for both types
      of OAuth tokens.\nL21: ## cite6†Delete an app authorization L22: OAuth and GitHub
      application owners can revoke a grant for their application and a specific user. You must
      provide a valid OAuth `access_token` as an input parameter and the grant for the token's owner
      will be deleted. Deleting an application's grant will also delete all OAuth tokens associated
      with the application for the user.\nL23: Once deleted, the application will have no access to
      the user's account and will no longer be listed on cite7†the application authorizations
      settings screen within GitHub†github.com .\nL24: ### cite8†Basic authentication for
      \"Delete an app authorization\" L25: \nL26: You must use cite9†Basic Authentication to use
      this endpoint. Use the application's `client_id` as the username and the `client_secret` as
      the password.\nL27: ### cite10†Parameters for \"Delete an app authorization\" L28: \nL29:
      Headers\nL30: `accept` string Setting to `application/vnd.github+json` is recommended.\nL31:
      Path parameters\nL32: `client_id` string Required The client ID of the GitHub app.\nL33: Body
      parameters\nL34: `access_token` string Required The OAuth access token used to authenticate to
      the GitHub API.\nL35: ### cite11†HTTP response status codes for \"Delete an app
      authorization\" L36: \nL37: Status code | Description\nL38: --- | ---\nL39: `204` | No
      Content\nL40: `422` | Validation failed, or the endpoint has been spammed.\nL41: \nL42: ###
      cite12†Code samples for \"Delete an app authorization\" L43: #### Request example\nL44:
      \nL45: delete/applications/{client_id}/grant\nL46: \nL47: * cURL\nL48: \nL49: *
      JavaScript\nL50: \nL51: Copy to clipboard curl request example\nL52: \nL53: `curl -L \\ -X
      DELETE \\ -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\ -u
      \"<YOUR_CLIENT_ID>:<YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET>\" \\ -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\
      https://api.github.com/applications/Iv1.8a61f9b3a7aba766/grant \\ -d
      '{\"access_token\":\"e72e16c7e42f292c6912e7710c838347ae178b4a\"}'`\nL54: \nL55: ####\nL56:
      \nL57: Response\nL58: \nL59: `Status: 204`\nL60: ## cite13†Check a token L61: \nL62: OAuth
      applications and GitHub applications with OAuth authorizations can use this API method for
      checking OAuth token validity without exceeding the normal rate limits for failed login
      attempts. Authentication works differently with this particular endpoint. Invalid tokens will
      return `404 NOT FOUND`.\nL63: ### cite14†Basic authentication for \"Check a token\" L64:
      \nL65: You must use cite9†Basic Authentication to use this endpoint. Use the application's
      `client_id` as the username and the `client_secret` as the password.\nL66: \nL67: ###
      cite15†Parameters for \"Check a token\" L68: \nL69: Headers\nL70: `accept` string Setting
      to `application/vnd.github+json` is recommended.\nL71: Path parameters\nL72: `client_id`
      string Required The client ID of the GitHub app.\nL73: Body parameters\nL74: `access_token`
      string Required The access_token of the OAuth or GitHub application.\nL75: ### cite16†HTTP
      response status codes for \"Check a token\" L76: \nL77: Status code | Description\nL78: --- |
      ---\nL79: `200` | OK\nL80: `404` | Resource not found\nL81: `422` | Validation failed, or the
      endpoint has been spammed.\nL82: \nL83: ### cite17†Code samples for \"Check a token\" L84:
      #### Request example\nL85: \nL86: post/applications/{client_id}/token\nL87: \nL88: *
      cURL\nL89: \nL90: * JavaScript\nL91: \nL92: Copy to clipboard curl request example\nL93:
      \nL94: `curl -L \\ -X POST \\ -H \"Accept: application/vnd.github+json\" \\ -u
      \"<YOUR_CLIENT_ID>:<YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET>\" \\ -H \"X-GitHub-Api-Version: 2026-03-10\" \\
      https://api.github.com/applications/Iv1.8a61f9b3a7aba766/token \\ -d
      '{\"access_token\":\"e72e16c7e42f292c6912e7710c838347ae178b4a\"}'`\nL95: ####\nL96: \nL97:
      Response\nL98: \nL99: * Example response\nL100: \nL101: * Response schema\nL102: \nL103:
